# DESCRIPTION

k1-syscon drives two of the SpacemiT K1's system controllers, the units that gate its devices' clocks and hold them in reset: the APBC, at `0xd4015000`, for the peripherals on the APB (the UARTs, I2C, PWM, GPIO, SPI), and the APMU, at `0xd4282800`, for the application subsystem's (the EMACs, SDHCIs, USB, PCIe, DMA, display).
devmgr(8) starts it for each, before the devices that link to it, by its record:

```text
driver k1-syscon
class clock
match dt spacemit,k1-syscon-apbc
match dt spacemit,k1-syscon-apmu
match dt spacemit,k1-pll
```

It also reads the K1's PLL unit, at `0xd4090000`, and changes nothing there.
The unit has three PLLs, each with three registers: SWCR1, its configuration; SWCR2, its outputs' gates (bit *k*-1 for output D*k*, PLL2's and PLL3's); and SWCR3, its feedback and its enable bit.
A PLL's rate is its SWCR1 and SWCR3 matched against the chip's table of configurations: PLL2's 3000 MHz, and PLL3's 1600, 1800, 2000, 2457.6, 3000 and 3200 MHz.
A PLL that matches none, or isn't enabled, is unknown and its outputs off, except PLL1, whose one rate, 2457.6 MHz, every peripheral clock assumes: it is taken at that whatever its registers say.
On the Orange Pi RV2 PLL2 runs at 3000 MHz with only D8 on, and PLL3 at 3200 MHz with only D2 on; PLL1's registers are the table's but for its enable bit, which reads clear.
The APMU's clocks have these outputs as parents, so devmgr(8) gives the APMU a link to the PLL unit, `clock pll`, starts the PLL unit's driver first, and the APMU's driver reads its `rates` when it starts; without it, the APMU knows PLL1 alone.
A device's `clocks` and `resets` name the unit and an id, the ids of `dt-bindings/clock/spacemit,k1-syscon.h`: the K1's first Ethernet MAC has the APMU's clock 37 (`emac0_bus`) and reset 35.
devmgr gives the unit's tree to that device's driver at `/dev/provider/<service>`, and its `/dev/dt/links` says which ids are the device's (devmgr(8)).

A clock is a gate, a mask of bits in its unit's control register: `on` sets them all, `off` clears them.
A clock with no gate (a fixed factor, a mux or a divider alone, such as the APMU's `pmua_aclk`) is always on.
The APMU's three SDHCI clocks, 11 to 13 (`sdh0`, `sdh1`, `sdh2`), also take a rate: each is one of its parents divided by 1 to 8, and `rate` picks the first parent and divider that make exactly the rate asked, sets them with the unit's frequency-change bit, and waits up to 5 ms for the hardware to clear it.
Their parents are PLL1 (2457.6 MHz) divided by 6, 4, 11, 13 or 23 (and 3 for `sdh2`), and PLL2's D8 and D5 (`sdh0` and `sdh1`; `sdh2` D8 alone), which `rate` uses where the PLL unit says the output is on: 204.8 MHz is PLL1's 409.6 MHz halved, and on the Orange Pi RV2 375 MHz is PLL2's D8.
The CPU clusters' core clocks, 2 (`cpu_c0_core`) and 6 (`cpu_c1_core`), take a rate too, for power(8): their mux, with no divider, has PLL1 divided by 4, 3, 6, 5 and 2 (614.4, 819.2, 409.6, 491.52 and 1228.8 MHz), PLL3's D3, PLL2's D3 (1 GHz), and the cluster's HI source, PLL3's D2 while the register's bit 13 is clear and its D1 while set, each used where the PLL unit says it is on: on the Orange Pi RV2, 1.6 GHz is HI, PLL3's D2, and 1 GHz can't be made, PLL2's D3 being off; the change is made with bit 12, the cluster's frequency-change bit, waited for in the same way, and bit 13 is never written.
The voltage a rate needs is the supply's, which this driver doesn't set.
A reset is asserted by writing its assert bits under its mask and released by writing its release bits: most of the APBC's are a reset bit, set while asserted, and most of the APMU's a release bit, clear while asserted.
Each change reads the register and writes it back with only those bits changed, one command at a time.
The APMU also holds each Ethernet MAC's control register, at `0x3e4` for the first and `0x3ec` for the second, which the MAC's node names by its `spacemit,apmu` offset, and after it the MAC's delay lines: `emac` sets the MAC's interface, RGMII or RMII, its reference and transmit clocks taken from the PHY, and for RGMII each delay line on with a code in steps of 15.6 ps (k1-emac(3) gives its node's `tx-internal-delay-ps` and `rx-internal-delay-ps`).
The first MAC's bus clock and reset are bits of that register too, so they are written only here.
It holds each PCIe controller's control register too, at `0x3cc`, `0x3d4` and `0x3dc`, which the controller's node names by its `spacemit,apmu` offset, and its control logic's after it: `pcie` sets or clears one of the bits k1-pcie(3) drives, the LTSSM's enable (`ltssm`), auxiliary power present (`aux-power`), PERST# asserted (`perst`), the PHY held in reset (`hold-phy`), root complex mode (`rc`) and the controller's soft reset (`soft-reset`); and `combo` gives the combo PHY to PCIe 0 or to USB 3 (k1-pcie-phy(3)).
The APBC holds the keys to the AIB's protected registers, among them the pads' I/O power domains: `unlock` writes its two, ASFAR then ASSAR, which allow the next such access, as k1-pinctrl(3) makes one to set the SD card's pads to 1.8 V.
A key is written whole, without reading the register first.
The APBC has 102 clocks and 51 resets, the APMU 62 clocks and 53 resets.

# FILES

The tree is served in devmgr(8)'s, at `drivers/k1-syscon-dt-system-controller@d4282800` for the APMU and `drivers/k1-syscon-dt-clock-controller@d4090000` for the PLL unit, and at `/dev/provider/<service>` in the drivers of the devices that link to it.

: `ctl`
  Takes a command a line: `clock <id> on|off`, `clock <id> rate <hz>`, `reset <id> assert|deassert`, `emac <offset> rgmii|rmii <tx> <rx>`, a MAC's control register and its delay codes (0 to 255), `pcie <offset> ltssm|aux-power|perst|hold-phy|rc|soft-reset on|off`, `combo pcie|usb`, or `unlock` (the APBC's), numbers in decimal or `0x` hexadecimal.
  A line it can't parse, an id or a MAC's or PCIe controller's offset the unit doesn't have, `unlock` to the APMU, `off` for a clock with no gate, or a rate no parent and divider make exactly fails the write as invalid, as does a rate change the hardware doesn't finish, and the driver logs which.
: `status`
  Read only.
  The unit, `apmu: 62 clocks, 53 resets`; then a line for each clock and each reset as its register reads now, `clock 37 emac0_bus on` (`off`, or `ungated` for one with no gate) and `reset 35 emac0 deasserted` (or `asserted`); then a line for each SDHCI and cluster core clock's rate as its mux and divider read now, `rate 11 sdh0 204800000` and `rate 2 cpu_c0_core 1600000000` (or `unknown`, from a PLL output whose rate isn't known or which is off); then a line for each MAC, `emac 0x3e4 rgmii tx 0 rx 0`; then a line for each PCIe controller, the bits `pcie` sets that are set, `pcie 0x3d4 perst hold-phy rc` (`-` for none), and the combo PHY's, `combo pcie` or `combo usb`; then `commands <n>, failed <n>`, and `last failure: <command>: <why>`, the why `no such id`, `no gate`, `no parent and divider make that rate` or `the change didn't take`; the APMU's ends with `plls from <service>`, or `plls assumed: PLL1 alone`.
  The PLL unit's: `pll: 3 PLLs`, then each PLL's registers and what they make, `pll3 swcr1 0x50dd67 swcr2 0x2 swcr3 0xc3eaaaab: 3200000000 Hz` (`unknown`, `off`, or PLL1's `taken as 2457600000 Hz`), then `commands <n>, failed <n>`.
: `rates`
  The PLL unit's, read only: `pll<n> <hz> on` for each PLL (`- off` where its rate is unknown), then `pll<n>_d<k> <hz> on|off` for each of PLL2's and PLL3's outputs D1 to D8.

